Example 11.14


Let the continuous random variable \(X\) have the following PDF:
\[
f_X(x) =
\begin{cases}
(a+1)(a+2) \ x^a (1-x) &\text{for } x \in (0, 1) \\
0 &\text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
Let \(g(X) = X^2\). What is \(\text{E}[g(X)] = \text{E}[X^2]\)?




\[
\begin{align}
\text{E}[X^2] &= \int_0^1 x^2 \cdot (a+1)(a+2) \ x^a (1-x) \ dx  \\
&= (a+1)(a+2) \int_0^1 x^{a+2} - x^{a+3} \ dx \\
&= (a+1)(a+2) \left[\frac{1}{a+3} x^{a+3} - \frac{1}{a+4}x^{a+4}\right]_0^1 = \frac{(a+1)(a+2)}{(a+3)(a+4)}
\end{align}
\]

Linear Functions of Standard Normal RVs (Proof)
We have that \(g^{-1}(z) = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\) so then:
\[
\begin{align}
f_X(x) &= f_Z(g^{-1}(x))\left| \frac{d}{dx} g^{-1}(x) \right| \\
&=  \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-\left(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\right)^2 / 2} \left| \frac{d}{dx} \left(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\right) \right| \\
&=  \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-\left(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\right)^2 / 2} \cdot \frac{1}{\sigma} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \sigma^2}} e^{-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\left(x-\mu\right)^2}
\end{align}
\]

